The wind on the Isle of Solitude did not just blow; it screamed. It was a constant, low-frequency hum that Elias had come to associate with the rhythm of his own heartbeat. For thirty years, he had lived in the lighthouse, a solitary sentinel against the gray, churning sea. His world was defined by the beam’s rotation, the creak of the iron stairs, and the smell of salt and old oil.

Elias was a man of few words and fewer visitors. The supply boat came once a month, dropping off canned goods and fuel, and the captain never lingered. Elias preferred it that way. He had learned to find comfort in the silence, a silence that was only ever broken by the storm.

The letter arrived on a Tuesday, tucked between a bag of flour and a crate of lanterns. It was not the usual government correspondence or the bills from the mainland. This was a thick, cream-colored envelope, heavy with the weight of paper, addressed in a neat, cursive hand that looked strangely familiar, though Elias could not recall seeing it before.

